{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1329,
    "dDurationMs": 5371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every second, thousands of cosmic rays - mostly\nhydrogen and helium nuclei - strike every"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6700,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "square meter of the earth’s upper atmosphere\n. We don’t really know where they come from,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10830,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but we do know that when cosmic rays crash\ninto air molecules in the atmosphere, they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15130,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "create a shower of other fundamental particles:\npions, kaons, positrons, electrons, neutrons,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20740,
    "dDurationMs": 2850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutrinos, gamma and X rays, and muons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23590,
    "dDurationMs": 3939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know this because we have particle detectors\nin labs down on the surface that detect the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27529,
    "dDurationMs": 4701,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "directions and energies of the particles in\nthese showers, and use them to study the original"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32230,
    "dDurationMs": 1110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmic rays."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33340,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s something fascinating about\nthe fact that we detect a lot of the muons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37160,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from cosmic rays down on the surface of the\nearth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39940,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because muons, if you make them in a laboratory,\nonly have a 1.5 microsecond half life before"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45080,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they spontaneously decay into an electron\nor positron and some neutrinos."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48760,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Oh yeah, the greek symbol, mu is both used\nfor “muon” AND for “microsecond”,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53660,
    "dDurationMs": 3810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which can certainly be a little confusing;\nbut the lifetime of muons is really close"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57470,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to a microsecond, so it’s also kind of beautifully\nappropriate/fitting."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60430,
    "dDurationMs": 2791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anyway, the point is that if you have a bunch\nof muons, More specifically, if you have a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63221,
    "dDurationMs": 4639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bunch of muons, you’ll only be left with\nabout 50% after 1.5 microseconds, and 25%"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67860,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after 3 microseconds, and after 10 microseconds\nthere will only be 0.1% of the muons left."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73210,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Muons don’t live very long -2.2 microseconds\non average!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77760,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To put that into perspective, light, which\ntravels fast enough that it could go around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82360,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the earth 7 times in a second, only travels\n660 meters, or less than half a mile, in 2.2"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89280,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "microseconds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90280,
    "dDurationMs": 3129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So even muons traveling at essentially the\nspeed of lighta , wouldn’t make it more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93409,
    "dDurationMs": 4901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than a kilometer or two before the vast majority\nof them decayed . Which is far less than the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98310,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "10 or 20 or 30 kilometers that muons DO regularly\ntravel from the upper atmosphere to the ground."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104200,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how do muons travel dozens of kilometers\nthrough the atmosphere without spontaneously"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108380,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decaying, when in fact they should only be\nable to travel less than one kilometer?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112640,
    "dDurationMs": 1510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Time dilation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114150,
    "dDurationMs": 4630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yes - because the muons are traveling close\nto the speed of light, their time literally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118780,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "passes more slowly - at a speed of 99.5% the\nspeed of light, 2.2 microseconds for them"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124810,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would be ~22 microseconds for us , enough\ntime for the average muon to travel at least"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129910,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "6km (instead of half of a kilometer) before\ndecaying."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133070,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And even higher-energy muons going even faster\nwould even more easily reach our detectors"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137900,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the earth’s surface before they decayed\n- at 99.995% the speed of light, the average"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143070,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "muon would live for 220 microseconds and travel\nat least 66 kilometers before decaying."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148770,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So from our perspective, the fact that so\nmany cosmic ray muons reach our detectors"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152900,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the earth’s surface is direct evidence\nfor special relativity and time dilation!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158150,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what about from the muons’ perspectives,\nwhere they DO only live on average 2.2 microseconds?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162970,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, for them the answer to the apparent\nparadox is also relativistic - relativistic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168420,
    "dDurationMs": 1480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "length contraction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169900,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From the muon’s perspective, it’s the\nearth and the atmosphere which are moving"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173380,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- at 99.995% the speed of light - towards\nthe muon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177670,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the lengths of moving objects are literally\ncontracted by a factor dependent on their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181650,
    "dDurationMs": 5899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speed - in this case, 50km of our atmosphere\nis, to the muon, literally only half a kilometer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187549,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- aka 500 meters - thick."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188549,
    "dDurationMs": 3711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which is thin enough for even a muon with\na lifetime of 2.2 microseconds to traverse"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192260,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- well, actually from this perspective the\natmosphere moves past the muon - but at a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196160,
    "dDurationMs": 3890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speed of 300 meters per microsecond and at\na distance of only 500 meters, the ground"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200050,
    "dDurationMs": 3350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has no problem reaching the muon before the\nmuon decays."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203400,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This, in my mind, is one of the most awesome\nexperimental verifications of special relativity:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208510,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the unequivocal time dilation (or length contraction,\ndepending on your perspective) for objects"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212970,
    "dDurationMs": 2099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moving close to the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215069,
    "dDurationMs": 8461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The \nspecific time dilation and length contraction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223530,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "factors I talked about can be calculated using\nthe time dilation and length contraction formulas"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228180,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- once you know how to use them, you can plug\nin any speed you want and see how much distances"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232190,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and time intervals will be distorted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234250,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And Brilliant.org, this video’s sponsor,\nis a great place to learn about not just the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238300,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "details of time dilation and length contraction,\nbut many of the other amazing equations that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242540,
    "dDurationMs": 1100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "describe our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243640,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like, they have a course that leads you towards\nunderstanding the Schrodinger equation of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247240,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum mechanics, and one on Hubble’s law\nin astronomy, and the famous Bayes’ theorem"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251920,
    "dDurationMs": 1899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of probability and statistics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253819,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the first 200 people who go to brilliant.org/minutephysics\nwill get 20% off a premium subscription to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259329,
    "dDurationMs": 3330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "brilliant with access to all of brilliant’s\ncourses and puzzles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262659,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Again, that’s brilliant.org/minutephysics\nfor a deeper understanding of the equations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267009,
    "dDurationMs": 3901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "(and not just the concepts) that underlie\nour universe."
    } ]
  } ]
}
